${banner}## beast-booter ${version}

${kind} ${tag}. Download the package for your system, unpack it and start **BeastBooter**
(docs/PORTABLE.md), or install from the boot files directly with
`beast-booter install --dist beast-booter-${version}-dist.zip`.

### Secure Boot certificate

On the first Secure Boot start, MokManager asks you to enroll
`ENROLL_THIS_KEY_IN_MOKMANAGER.cer` (also attached as
`beast-booter-${version}-ENROLL_THIS_KEY_IN_MOKMANAGER.cer`). Enroll it only if
**View key** shows exactly this SHA-1 fingerprint:

    ${cert_sha1}

- Subject: `${cert_subject}` (valid until ${cert_until})
- SHA-1: `${cert_sha1_colons}`
- SHA-256: `${cert_sha256}`

The procedure is in docs/SECURE_BOOT.md, section 3 "Check the key before you enroll it".

### Vulnerabilities

From `beast-booter-${version}-VULNERABILITIES.md` (SBOM scan with grype and osv-scanner, OpenVEX applied,
plus the boot-chain checks: SBAT, dbx, signing certificates):

- Open boot-chain findings: ${open_boot}
- Open findings in shipped software (drive, beast-net, portable runtimes): ${open_shipped}
- Open findings in build/test tooling (not shipped): ${open_build}
- Closed by VEX statements: ${closed}
- Release gate: ${gate}

Boot-chain findings and their VEX status:

${boot_findings}

Critical/high findings in shipped software: ${shipped_high}

### Verify the download

    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing   # Windows: Get-FileHash <file> -Algorithm SHA256${attest_line}

The SBOM (`beast-booter-${version}-sbom.cdx.json`, CycloneDX 1.6; `.spdx.json`, SPDX 2.3) lists every
file on the drive, the packages they come from, the beast-net kernel and initrd packages and the
Python runtimes in the portable packages. How releases are built and checked: docs/RELEASING.md.

